// Dates follow the “middle chronology”; the earliest (7th–9th centuries) come from Tibetan annals and inscriptions, Chinese Tang sources and the Dunhuang manuscripts, and the most remote are traditional and debated. BC/AD.
// Note on neutrality: relations between Tibet and China are treated as history, setting out the readings of different historiographies without taking a political position on the present.

const visao = [
  { caixa: 'In brief', texto: [
    '**Tibet** is the great plateau at the heart of Asia, more than 4,000 metres above sea level, between the Himalayas and the deserts of Central Asia. It was there that, in the 7th century, the kings of the **Yarlung** valley united the tribes and small kingdoms of the plateau and created the **Tibetan Empire** (c. 618–842), a power that at its height contested Central Asia with Tang China, the Turks and the Arabs. Its first great king, **Songtsen Gampo**, is remembered as the founder of the state, of Lhasa and of the Tibetan script; one of his successors, **Trisong Detsen**, as the king who founded the first Buddhist monastery, **Samye**.',
    'The empire fell in 842, after the assassination of King Langdarma, but Tibet did not vanish: it reinvented itself as a **monastic civilisation**. From the 11th century onwards, Tibetan Buddhism, in lineages of masters and schools (Nyingma, Kagyu, Sakya, later Gelug), organised life on the plateau. The **Sakya** ruled Tibet under the protection of the Mongol emperors (the Yuan dynasty) in the 13th century; the **Dalai Lamas**, of the Gelug school, became spiritual leaders and, from 1642, political ones too, with the **Fifth Dalai Lama** and the building of the **Potala Palace**. This site tells that story down to the 18th century.'
  ] },
  { img: 'tib-mapa-imperio', leg: 'Map of the Tibetan Empire at its greatest extent, 8th–9th centuries; the borders are approximate and varied greatly.' },
  { h: 'Where it was' },
  'Historic Tibet is the **Tibetan Plateau**, the highest and largest in the world: an average altitude near 4,500 m, with mountains, salt lakes, short-grass steppes and deep valleys. To the south stands the wall of the **Himalayas** (with Everest, which Tibetans call **Chomolungma**); to the north, the Taklamakan desert and the Kunlun mountains; to the east, the gorges through which the great rivers of Asia run (the Yangtze, the Mekong, the Salween). The **Yarlung Tsangpo** (later the Brahmaputra), the Indus and the Sutlej also rise here, which is why the plateau is sometimes called the “water tower” of Asia.',
  'Tibetans lived mainly in two worlds: the **fertile valleys of the centre and south** (Yarlung, Lhasa, Shigatse), where barley was grown, and the **high lands of the north and west**, where **nomads** herded yaks, sheep and goats. Over history, the territory under Tibetan rule varied greatly. The regions of **Ü** and **Tsang** (centre) and **Kham** and **Amdo** (east) are traditionally considered the Tibetan cultural area, but the political limits of each period changed, and are debated.',
  { img: 'tib-planalto', leg: 'Landscape of the Tibetan Plateau, with a mountain range and lake' },
  { h: 'When it existed' },
  'Tibetan history is usually divided into **four great phases**: the empire (7th–9th centuries), the time of fragmentation and the “second diffusion” of Buddhism (9th–13th centuries), the time of schools and regional powers (13th–16th centuries) and the time of the Dalai Lamas (from the 17th century). The dates below are approximate, and the oldest are the most uncertain.',
  { tabela: { cab: ['Phase', 'Approximate dates', 'What marks it'], linhas: [
    ['Early kingdoms and Yarlung', 'until c. 618', 'Small kingdoms and chiefdoms of the plateau; Zhangzhung in the west (Bön religion); the dynasty of the kings of Yarlung, partly legendary'],
    ['Tibetan Empire', 'c. 618 – 842', 'Songtsen Gampo, the script, Lhasa; expansion into Central Asia; Trisong Detsen and Samye; the treaty of 821/822; Langdarma and the end of the empire'],
    ['Era of fragmentation', '842 – c. 1000', 'End of central power; regional princes and kingdoms (such as Guge, in the west); Buddhism survives in the peripheral regions'],
    ['Second diffusion and schools', 'c. 980 – 1240', 'Translators such as Rinchen Zangpo; arrival of Atisha (1042); Kagyu masters such as Marpa and Milarepa; foundation of Sakya (1073)'],
    ['The Sakya and the Mongols', 'c. 1244 – 1354', 'Sakya Pandita and Phagpa; relations with the Mongols and the Yuan dynasty; Sakya administration over much of central Tibet'],
    ['Phagmodrupa and Rinpung', '1354 – c. 1565', 'Changchub Gyaltsen; Tsongkhapa and the Gelug school; the monasteries of Ganden, Drepung and Sera; rivalries of princes and schools'],
    ['Tsangpa and Dalai Lamas', 'c. 1565 – 18th century', 'The title of Dalai Lama (1578); Güshi Khan and the Fifth Dalai Lama (1642); the Potala; intervention of the Qing dynasty from 1720']
  ] } },
  { img: 'tib-esquema-poderes', leg: 'Drawn diagram of the great phases of power in Tibet, from the empire to the Dalai Lamas; the chronology is simplified.' },
  { h: 'Who were the Tibetans?' },
  'The **Tibetans** are the people who speak the languages and dialects of Tibetan, of the **Tibeto-Burman** family. In their own language they call the country **Bod** (the name “Tibet” comes from Turkic and Arabic forms, of debated origin). Their ancestors were hunters, herders and farmers of the plateau, and anthropologists have shown that over millennia they adapted genetically and culturally to life at altitude, with less oxygen. The written Tibetan language, formalised in the 7th century, gave them a **common identity**, despite the diversity of dialects and local powers.',
  { h: 'Why they matter' },
  { lista: [
    '**An empire at altitude:** the Tibetan Empire was one of the great powers of Asia from the 7th to the 9th century, and its inscriptions, annals and treaties show a complex administration.',
    '**Tibetan Buddhism:** one of the great traditions of Buddhism, with its own philosophy, rituals, art and monasteries, which shaped Tibet, Mongolia and the Himalayas.',
    '**A civilisation of the book:** from the hundreds of volumes of the **Kangyur** and **Tengyur** (the Buddhist canon) to the translation of thousands of texts from Sanskrit, Tibetan preserved works that were lost in India.',
    '**An original system of government:** power transmitted through lineages of **reincarnated masters** (*tulku*) and the union of spiritual and political power in the hands of the Dalai Lamas.',
    '**Art and architecture:** the fortress-monasteries, the *thangka* (paintings on cloth), the mandalas, the Potala.',
    '**Medicine, astrology and contemplative arts:** traditions of their own, now studied and practised all over the world.',
    '**A laboratory of history:** the relations of Tibet with China, the Mongols, India and Nepal are a case study in power, religion and borders, and are still debated.'
  ] },
  { img: 'tib-potala-hoje', leg: 'Potala Palace, Lhasa' },
  { img: 'tib-bandeiras-oracao', leg: 'Prayer flags (lung ta) in the Himalayas' },
  { caixa: 'Tibet today', texto: 'Historic Tibet is today administered by the **People’s Republic of China**, largely as the **Tibet Autonomous Region** (created in 1965), together with Tibetan areas in neighbouring provinces (Qinghai, Sichuan, Gansu, Yunnan). More than six million Tibetans live in China. The 14th Dalai Lama, **Tenzin Gyatso**, has lived in exile in India since 1959, and there is an important Tibetan diaspora. **Readings of Tibetan history and of Tibet’s relations with China** differ according to authors and governments, and are summarised, with their sources, in the “Legacy” section. This site tells the history without taking a political position on the present.' }
];

const linha = [
  'This timeline follows the main events of Tibetan history down to the 18th century. Many dates of the first period come from the **Old Tibetan Annals** and Tibetan chronicles, and from Chinese Tang sources; they do not always agree. Tibetan chronicles written centuries later, such as those of **Sakya** and of the **Dalai Lamas**, mix history and religious tradition. Where there is doubt, it is flagged.',
  { linha: [
    { d: 'Tradition: 2nd century BC; history: until c. AD 600', t: 'The kings of Yarlung', x: 'Tibetan tradition, written many centuries later, begins the list of kings with **Nyatri Tsenpo**, who is said to have descended from the sky into the **Yarlung** valley (in southern central Tibet), on a traditional date in the 2nd century BC. This is legend, not documented history. What the earliest sources allow us to say is that in the 6th century a clan of Yarlung chiefs began to unite its neighbours, and that central Tibet was then a set of kingdoms and chiefdoms, alongside the kingdom of **Zhangzhung** in the west, where the **Bön** religion was practised.' },
    { d: 'c. 618 – 629', t: 'Namri Songtsen', x: 'The Yarlung chief **Namri Songtsen** conquers his neighbours in central Tibet and sends a first embassy to China. According to tradition he was poisoned by enemies (c. 629), and his son, still young, **Songtsen Gampo**, restored the family’s power.' },
    { d: 'c. 629 – 650', t: 'Songtsen Gampo', x: 'The true founder of the empire. He defeats his internal enemies, moves the court to **Lhasa** (then called *Rasa*; the date of the move is debated), subdues the kingdom of **Zhangzhung** (c. 644–645) and sends embassies to China and Nepal. The dates of his birth and reign vary in the sources (c. 604–650 is one of those in use).' },
    { d: 'c. 640', t: 'The Tibetan script', x: 'According to tradition, the king sent the minister **Thonmi Sambhota** to India to study writing and grammar, and he created the Tibetan alphabet, adapted from an Indian script. The story of Thonmi is known from late sources, and there are doubts about the details. What is certain is that the Tibetan alphabet existed in the 7th–8th centuries and is of Indian origin.' },
    { d: '641', t: 'The royal marriages', x: 'According to Tibetan and Chinese sources, the king married the Nepalese princess **Bhrikuti** and the Chinese princess **Wencheng** (who arrived in Tibet in 641). Tradition says that each brought an image of the Buddha and that, to receive them, the **Jokhang** and the **Ramoche** were built in Lhasa. The Old Tibetan Annals speak of Wencheng later and in less detail; the exact role of the princesses in the nascent Buddhism is debated.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'tib-songtsen-gampo-estatua', leg: 'Statue of Songtsen Gampo, Tibet' },
  { img: 'tib-tumulos-chongye', leg: 'Tombs of the Tibetan kings, Chongye (Qonggyai) valley' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 650', t: 'Death of Songtsen Gampo', x: 'According to the chronicles, he died c. 649 or 650, still young. He was buried in a great tomb in the **Chongye** valley, where there are about ten large burial mounds of the kings of the empire; which king lies in which is debated. The kings and great men of the empire were buried with pre-Buddhist rites, and only later did Buddhist traditions stress the figure of Songtsen Gampo as the “king of the law”.' },
    { d: '670', t: 'Dafeichuan', x: 'Tibetan armies defeat a Chinese army of the Tang dynasty in the **Dafei** valley (the exact location is debated), and come to control the routes of the Tarim for decades. Tibet became one of the powers contesting the Silk Road, alongside the Tang, the Turks and, further west, the Arabs.' },
    { d: '710', t: 'Princess Jincheng', x: 'A second Chinese princess, **Jincheng**, marries the young king **Tridé Tsuktsen** (Mes Ag-tshoms), in a new alliance after decades of war and peace. In his time Buddhism gains support at court, but opposition from noble clans tied to the older cults remains strong.' },
    { d: 'c. 755 – 797', t: 'Trisong Detsen', x: 'The most powerful king of the empire. According to tradition he is the king who **protected Buddhism** and brought to court the Indian scholar **Shantarakshita** and the tantric master **Padmasambhava**. Under his reign the empire reaches its greatest extent: from the Tarim to Nepal and the borders of Sichuan and Yunnan.' },
    { d: '763', t: 'The capture of Chang’an', x: 'Taking advantage of the huge crisis opened in China by the **An Lushan** rebellion, a Tibetan army **occupies the Tang capital, Chang’an** (Xi’an), for about fifteen days, and briefly installs a puppet emperor. It was the empire’s greatest military feat, but they did not keep it.' },
    { d: 'c. 775 – 779', t: 'Samye', x: 'The building of the **monastery of Samye**, the first great Buddhist monastery in Tibet, began c. 775 and it was consecrated c. 779. Tradition says it was planned as a **mandala** in stone, with the central temple representing Mount Meru. The first Tibetan monks were ordained there. Buddhism became the religion of the state, largely against the resistance of part of the nobility.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'tib-exercito-imperial', leg: 'Horsemen of the Tibetan imperial army, 8th century; conjectural reconstruction. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { img: 'tib-debate-samye', leg: 'The debate of Samye, c. 792–794, according to tradition; imagined scene. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 792 – 794', t: 'The debate of Samye', x: 'According to later Tibetan chronicles, the king held a debate at Samye between the Indian monk **Kamalashila** (defender of the gradual path) and a Chinese Chan master, **Hoshang Moheyan** (defender of sudden enlightenment). According to Tibetan tradition Kamalashila won, and Tibet followed Indian Buddhism. Historians debate the historicity, the date and the outcome a great deal, because the Dunhuang manuscripts give different views.' },
    { d: 'c. 787 – 848', t: 'Tibetan Dunhuang', x: 'Tibetan troops conquer **Dunhuang**, a key oasis of the Silk Road, c. 786–787 (the exact date is debated), and rule it until the mid-9th century. In the Dunhuang caves, sealed around the year 1000 and opened in 1900, hundreds of **manuscripts in Old Tibetan** were left, including the **Annals** and the Chronicle of Tibet: the oldest and richest source on the empire.' },
    { d: '815 – 838', t: 'Ralpachen', x: 'King **Ralpachen** (Tri Ralpachen) protects Buddhism and orders the systematic translation of texts and the fixing of vocabulary. In **821/822**, Tibet and Tang China sign a **peace treaty**, whose text was carved on stone pillars, in Tibetan and in Chinese, in Lhasa, Chang’an and on the frontier. The pillar in front of the Jokhang still exists.' },
    { d: '838 – 842', t: 'Langdarma and the end of the empire', x: 'After the death (or murder) of Ralpachen, his brother **Langdarma** (Ü Dumtsen) comes to the throne and, according to Tibetan chronicles, persecuted the monasteries. He was **assassinated in 842** by a monk, and the empire dissolved in wars of succession between his heirs. Later Buddhist sources paint Langdarma as a persecutor, but historians note that these sources are hostile and that the crisis of the empire had political and economic causes.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'tib-pilar-lhasa', leg: 'Pillar of the Sino-Tibetan treaty, in front of the Jokhang temple, Lhasa, inscribed in 821/822.' },
  { img: 'tib-dunhuang-manuscrito', leg: 'Tibetan manuscript from Dunhuang, 8th–9th centuries, British Library / Bibliothèque nationale de France.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '842 – c. 1000', t: 'The era of fragmentation', x: 'Without a central power, Tibet divides. Descendants of the kings found regional kingdoms, such as **Guge-Purang** in the west. Buddhism disappears from the centre, but survives in Kham and Amdo and in the west. Tibetans call this time the “period of fragmentation” or the “dark age”.' },
    { d: 'c. 958 – 1042', t: 'The second diffusion of Buddhism', x: 'The king of Guge sends young men to Kashmir; the translator **Rinchen Zangpo** (958–1055) founds monasteries and translates texts. In **1042**, the Indian master **Atisha** (c. 982–1054) arrives in western Tibet and then in the centre, and gives rise to the **Kadam** school. Masters such as **Marpa** and **Milarepa** establish the **Kagyu** lineage.' },
    { d: '1073', t: 'Foundation of Sakya', x: 'Khön Könchok Gyalpo founds the monastery of **Sakya** (c. 1073; sources vary between 1071 and 1073), seat of the Khön family and of the **Sakya** school, which will give Tibet its rulers of the 13th century.' },
    { d: '1244 – 1247', t: 'Sakya Pandita and Godan', x: 'The Mongol prince **Godan**, a grandson of Genghis Khan, invites **Sakya Pandita** to his court (invitation in 1244; the meeting takes place c. 1247, in the Liangzhou region). The Sakya master accepts Mongol authority over Tibet and writes letters to the Tibetans telling them to submit. It was the beginning of the Sakya–Mongol link.' },
    { d: '1253 – 1280', t: 'Phagpa and Kublai Khan', x: 'Sakya Pandita’s nephew, **Phagpa** (1235–1280), meets **Kublai Khan** in 1253. In 1260 he is appointed **state preceptor** and later imperial preceptor; in 1264 the department is created which, renamed in 1288, was called the **Xuanzheng Yuan** (“Bureau of Buddhist and Tibetan Affairs”). In 1269 Phagpa creates the **’phags-pa script**, for official use in the Mongol empire. The Sakya come to govern central Tibet with Mongol support.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'tib-sakya-mosteiro', leg: 'Sakya Monastery, Tibet' },
  { img: 'tib-phagpa-kublai', leg: 'Phagpa and Kublai Khan: a Tibetan or Chinese depiction of the Yuan period.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '1290 – 1354', t: 'The end of Sakya power', x: 'In 1285–1290 a revolt of **Drikung** monastery is crushed by Yuan troops, with Sakya support. As the Yuan empire weakens, Sakya power declines. In **1354**, the chief **Changchub Gyaltsen**, of the Phagmodrupa clan, takes power and founds a dynasty that rules central Tibet for more than a century and aims to restore the old prestige of the time of the kings.' },
    { d: '1357 – 1419', t: 'Tsongkhapa and the Gelug', x: '**Tsongkhapa** is born (1357), a reformer who insists on monastic discipline and study. His disciples found **Ganden** (1409), **Drepung** (1416) and **Sera** (1419), great monastery-universities, and, in 1447, **Tashilhunpo**. The school that forms is the **Gelug** (“the virtuous”).' },
    { d: '1578', t: 'The title of Dalai Lama', x: 'The Mongol **Altan Khan** grants the title of **Dalai Lama** (“ocean lama”, from Mongolian *dalai*, “ocean”) to **Sonam Gyatso**, leader of the Gelug school. His two predecessors, already dead, came to be counted as the 1st and 2nd. Sonam Gyatso is therefore the 3rd Dalai Lama; the 4th was a Mongol, a great-grandson of Altan Khan.' },
    { d: '1624', t: 'The Jesuit Andrade at Tsaparang', x: 'The Portuguese Jesuit **António de Andrade** (1580–1634) sets out from Agra, in India, and reaches **Tsaparang**, capital of the kingdom of **Guge**, in western Tibet, in 1624. He was one of the first Europeans to describe Tibet. A mission was opened in 1625. The kingdom of Guge fell to Ladakh around 1630 (a debated date), and the mission ended in the following years.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'tib-andrade-tsaparang', leg: 'António de Andrade arrives at Tsaparang, 1624; imagined scene. AI-generated illustration.' },
  { img: 'tib-quinto-dalai-lama', leg: 'The Fifth Dalai Lama, Tibetan painting' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '1642 – 1682', t: 'The Fifth Dalai Lama and the Ganden Phodrang', x: 'In 1642, the Mongol chief **Güshi Khan**, who defeated the rivals of the Gelug school, hands power over Tibet to the **Fifth Dalai Lama**, **Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso** (1617–1682). The government called the **Ganden Phodrang** is born, and will last until the 20th century. In 1645 the rebuilding of the **Potala** begins, on a hill in Lhasa where, according to tradition, Songtsen Gampo’s palace stood. The White Palace was ready in 1648; the Red Palace only in 1694.' },
    { d: '1682 – 1706', t: 'A secret and a poet', x: 'The Fifth Dalai Lama dies in 1682, but his regent, **Desi Sangye Gyatso**, hides the death for about fourteen years, to finish the works at the Potala (the secret was revealed in 1696–97). The 6th Dalai Lama, **Tsangyang Gyatso** (1683–1706), was a poet who did not accept the monastic life, and died young in circumstances still discussed. The 7th, **Kelzang Gyatso** (1708–1757), was recognised in 1720.' },
    { d: '1717 – 1751', t: 'Dzungars and Qing', x: 'In 1717 an army of **Dzungars**, western Mongols, occupies Lhasa and is expelled in 1720 by an army sent by the **Qing** emperor **Kangxi**. From 1727 the Qing keep two representatives in Lhasa, the **ambans**; in 1751 the government is reorganised, with the Dalai Lama and a council, the **Kashag**. How to interpret this relationship is one of the most debated points in Tibetan history (see “Legacy”).' }
  ] }
];

